From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] drm/mediatek: run a path through several controllers and two pipes

The display of MT6897 has four controllers, a dispsys and an ovlsys for
each half of the screen, with their own routes, mutexes and power
domains. Only the driver of dispsys0 exists, and its path has both
halves.

- Add a table with the controller of each component of a path. The
  connections and the mutexes go to the controller of the component, and
  a connection across controllers to both of them. Each mutex of a pipe
  waits for the start of frame of its output. All the components power
  on for themselves, as they are not all in one domain.
- A path can have a second pipe. Each pipe takes half of the width. A
  plane goes to the same layer of both overlays, with its part of the
  screen: a plane in the middle shows in both, and the right part starts
  where the left part ends in memory. Flipping a plane would have to
  swap the parts, so it only supports no rotation.
- The vblank comes from the first overlay, and only the first link of
  the panel has an encoder. The CRTC does not wait for a vblank when it
  turns off, since the DSI has stopped the video before its last
  commands. What was pending goes away with it.
- This CRTC holds its power domains from its creation to the first
  commit. The bootloader's display runs on them, and removing the
  framebuffer driver would take them away from under its DMA. The first
  commit starts the outputs before anything else, so they stop at the
  end of a frame and the resets find nothing in flight.

Add the data of MT6897. There is no CMDQ driver for it yet, so the
registers go out from the vblank interrupt with the CPU. That interrupt
leaves a commit in the middle of its update for the next vblank, or its
event would go out without its planes. It does not configure the
components again: the hardware init had the same values, and a reset
from the vblank of the first pipe could hit the second one in its frame.
The aliases of the nodes tell the controllers apart.
